# Regulatory and Political Brief — Quiet-by-Design Standards for MWAA Terminals

**Author:** Regulatory-Political Analyst
**Run:** Quiet-by-Design Standards for MWAA Terminals
**Date:** 2026-08-07
**Length:** ~2,300 words

---

## Frame

A "quiet-by-design" standard is not primarily an acoustics problem. It is a codified operator standard that has to survive four layers of federal preemption, a signatory-carrier lease that MWAA just re-executed for fifteen years, a heightened DCA safety environment created by a fatal midair collision on January 29, 2025, and a bond market that is about to underwrite a $20–22.5B Dulles program. The engineering community will treat the constraint as NFPA 72 speech intelligibility and TSA security messaging. Those are floors. The binding ceilings are political and contractual, and they sit above the design manual, not inside it.

The good news for the Strategist: most of the binding forces here are on MWAA's side of the fence. The **MWAA Design Manual** has "the force of law on the Airport" for anyone designing or building on MWAA property [Source: https://www.mwaa.com/sites/mwaa.com/files/2021-09/01_DM_2020.pdf]. That is a policy lever a design-manual amendment can pull without new federal rulemaking, without airline consent for common-use standards, and without congressional action. The bad news: the pieces that touch **airline gate operations, TSA checkpoint messaging, CBP FIS communications, and DCA political optics** cannot be pulled that way, and the standard has to acknowledge them explicitly or it will collapse the first time a signatory carrier or a federal partner tests it.

---

## Key Findings

1. **The MWAA Design Manual is the vehicle. Nothing else is.** The 2020 Design Manual is described as "a mandatory guide" with "the force of law on the Airport" [Source: https://www.mwaa.com/sites/mwaa.com/files/2021-09/01_DM_2020.pdf]. A quiet-by-design standard adopted there governs every new-build and substantially renovated space at IAD and DCA without a board vote, without airline majority-in-interest approval, and without a rate-and-charge fight — provided it does not force capital cost increases that trigger MII review under the 2025 U&L.

2. **NFPA 72 sets a hard technical floor: STI ≥ 0.50 in required areas.** The 2022 edition of NFPA 72 requires a Speech Transmission Index of 0.50 minimum (Common Intelligibility Scale ~0.70) for voice evacuation systems in the areas the code designates [Source: https://www.suppressionsystems.com/voice-intelligibility-in-mass-notification-systems/]. Any "quiet" design that reduces reverberation control, PA power, or loudspeaker density below what commissioning-verified STI 0.50 requires is not just poor practice — it fails building code as adopted by Fairfax, Loudoun, and Arlington counties through MWAA's own building code adoption.

3. **The DCA 1,250-mile perimeter rule and slot cap are the canonical case of a policy Congress cannot move.** Section 502 of the FAA Reauthorization Act of 2024 (Pub. L. 118-63, enacted May 16, 2024) added 10 beyond-perimeter exemptions after H.R. 3185/S. 1933 had sought 56 [Source: https://www.everycrsreport.com/reports/IN12504.html]. That is the fifth congressional round of DCA slot bargaining (2000, 2003, 2012, 2018, 2024) and it produced a fractional change. **The lesson for a quiet-by-design standard: any element that requires an act of Congress to enforce will not be enforced on this decade's construction timeline.** Design around what MWAA controls.

4. **The DCA political environment after January 29, 2025 has raised the cost of any change that touches emergency communication.** Local officials, MWAA staff, and airline safety leaders have publicly framed the fatal midair collision as a factor in DCA operational and safety decisions going forward (context packet, §7). Reducing announcements at DCA — even those that are demonstrably ineffective — is now a political act that will attract NTSB, congressional, and press attention. IAD does not carry that overhang. **The standard must permit divergent DCA/IAD announcement policies or it will get rejected on DCA political grounds and drag the IAD case down with it.**

5. **ADA effective-communication rules require visual counterparts, and DOT recently tightened them.** 49 CFR § 27.71(i) now requires airport operators to "enable or ensure high-contrast captioning at all times on all televisions and other audio-visual displays" in gate, ticketing, lounge, and common terminal areas at airports with 10,000+ annual enplanements [Source: https://www.corada.com/documents/49-cfr-part-27/subpart-b-accessibility-requirements-in-specific-operating-administration-programs-airports-railroads-and-highways]. A quiet standard that leans on visual channels must fund the visual channels; the regulation makes the caption side non-optional.

6. **The PFC cap of $4.50 has been frozen since 2000 and every FAA reauthorization since has kept it there.** The last statutory increase was in the Wendell H. Ford Aviation Investment and Reform Act signed in 2000, raising the cap from $3 to $4.50 [Source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Passenger_facility_charge] [Source: https://reason.org/wp-content/uploads/modernizing-passenger-facility-charge.pdf]. This is not a lever available inside the fifteen-year U&L horizon. Any capital element of a quiet-by-design standard has to be funded from bond proceeds, AIP formula and discretionary grants, BIL Airport Terminal Program dollars, or the airline rate base — not from PFC expansion. Assume the ceiling stays at $4.50 through the next reauthorization (FY2029) and probably longer.

7. **The 2025–2039 Use and Lease Agreement is a fifteen-year contract lock that the standard must fit inside.** The current Airport Use Agreement and Premises Lease took effect January 1, 2025 and runs through calendar 2039, alongside a $9.0B joint capital plan (~$7.0B IAD, ~$2.39B DCA) (context packet, §3). Any quiet-by-design element that raises airline-recoverable costs materially will run into majority-in-interest review; the specific MII thresholds in the 2025 U&L are an unresolved gap and should be verified before publication. **The Strategist should distinguish sharply between (a) elements MWAA can adopt without airline MII approval and (b) elements that require it.**

8. **Historic preservation binds the Saarinen main terminal and DCA Terminal 1, and it binds through Section 106 whenever federal funds or approvals are present.** The original Saarinen main terminal is on the National Register (listed 1997), and MWAA has expanded IAD under a Programmatic Memorandum of Agreement (PMOA) with the Virginia SHPO tied to the 1964 Saarinen master plan [Source: https://www.achp.gov/sites/default/files/2021-03/2020_FAA_Section3_ProgressReport.pdf] [Source: https://parametric-architecture.com/trumps-22-5-billion-dulles-airport]. Any interior acoustic intervention in the main terminal envelope — new absorptive treatments, ceiling changes, loudspeaker replacements — should be assumed to trigger PMOA consultation on schedule critical paths.

