## Evidence — Organized by Binding Force

### 1. What is technically fixed (federal safety and accessibility floors)

**NFPA 72 (2022), Chapter 18 (Notification Appliances) and Chapter 24 (Emergency Communications Systems).** Enforced through local building code adoption. Speech intelligibility in required areas must meet STI ≥ 0.50, verified per Annex D methodology (STIPA measurement) [Source: https://www.suppressionsystems.com/voice-intelligibility-in-mass-notification-systems/] [Source: https://bedrock-elite.com/applications/alarm-paga-testing]. **What this binds:** any zone the code designates as requiring voice evacuation must be commissioned to STI 0.50 with documented STIPA results. "Quiet" cannot mean fewer or weaker EVACS loudspeakers; it can mean absorptive treatments that improve STI at the same or lower sound-pressure level.

**14 CFR Part 139 (§ 139.325).** Requires an Airport Emergency Plan that includes "a communications network" capable of prompt notification, per FAA AC 150/5200-31C. **Part 139 does not prescribe PA sound-pressure levels, STI thresholds, or paging frequency** for terminal announcements. This is important: Part 139 is not the reason terminals are loud. Operator policy and NFPA 72 are.

**49 CFR § 27.71 — Airport facilities (DOT ADA rule).** Applies to every DOT-financial-assistance recipient at a commercial service airport. Subsection (i) requires high-contrast captioning at all times on all TVs and audio-visual displays in gate, ticketing, lounge, and common terminal areas at 10,000+ enplanement airports [Source: https://www.corada.com/documents/49-cfr-part-27/subpart-b-accessibility-requirements-in-specific-operating-administration-programs-airports-railroads-and-highways] [Source: https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-49/subtitle-A/part-27/subpart-B/section-27.71]. The Air Carrier Access Act (14 CFR Part 382) adds airline-side obligations for effective communication at gates, including visual counterparts to audible flight information. **What this binds:** any "muted televisions" element must specify captioning, not muting alone; any "visual-first" element still requires audible counterparts for blind and low-vision passengers, and vice versa.

**TSA required security messaging.** TSA retains authority over checkpoint messaging and security announcements; airport-general security announcements (e.g., unattended-bag reminders) are operator-implemented against TSA guidance. Current TSA guidance on cadence, wording, and multilingual coverage was not verifiable in this pass and is a **named research gap.** **What this binds:** any announcement-governance standard has to route non-emergency TSA messaging through a TSA-approved script and cannot unilaterally suppress it.

**CBP FIS-hall communication.** CBP governs communication and signage inside the sterile international arrivals process at the IAD FIS. Anything airside of the FIS entry doors — including announcement policy inside the sterile hall — needs CBP concurrence, not just MWAA approval. **What this binds:** the IAD international-arrivals experience cannot be standardized purely by MWAA design manual language.

### 2. What is politically fixed (Congress-set, decade-plus half-lives)

**DCA slot and perimeter rule (49 U.S.C. § 41714 and FAA reauthorization additions).** Section 502 of Pub. L. 118-63 added 10 beyond-perimeter exemptions in 2024, against a bill (H.R. 3185/S. 1933) that had sought 56 [Source: https://www.everycrsreport.com/reports/IN12504.html]. MWAA officially opposed the expansion, arguing "the DC region is well served by the bigger IAD, which is located about 30 miles west" [Source: https://www.everycrsreport.com/reports/IN12504.html]. **Relevance to quiet-by-design:** the DCA traffic profile is now marginally busier per gate than it was in 2023, and the marginal beyond-perimeter flights concentrate at peaks. Announcement density at DCA Terminal 2 gates will not go down on its own.

**PFC cap frozen at $4.50 since 2000.** Every FAA reauthorization since (2003, 2012, 2018, 2024) has left it there [Source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Passenger_facility_charge]. ACI-NA and airport lobbies have been pressing DOT and Congress for an increase since at least 2017 [Source: https://aviationweek.com/air-transport/airports-networks/aci-na-meeting-dot-push-pfc-increase]. **Relevance:** do not assume PFC-funded terminal enhancements as a way to underwrite quiet-by-design capital. The next realistic window is the FY2029 reauthorization cycle, i.e., after Concourse E opens and while the expanded Dulles program is in its early construction years.

**Federal AIP formula/discretionary split and BIL aviation dollars.** AIP formula grants at large hubs top out well below what a $20–22.5B program consumes; the discretionary tranche is competitive and politically allocated. BIL's Airport Terminal Program (FY22–FY26 appropriations) provided a supplemental terminal-specific pot. **Relevance:** federal terminal grants prefer visible passenger-experience elements. Acoustics, absorption, and zoned PA infrastructure are grant-fundable when packaged inside a broader accessibility or modernization narrative; specifying them as free-standing "quiet" upgrades reads as amenity spend and grades poorly.

**Metropolitan Washington Airports Act of 1986 (49 U.S.C. §§ 49101–49112).** MWAA is a 17-member board authority: 7 Virginia, 4 D.C., 3 federal, 3 Maryland [Source: https://uscode.house.gov/view.xhtml?req=granuleid:USC-prelim-title49-section49106&num=0&edition=prelim]. Federal appointees (Presidential, with Senate advice and consent) create a political vector that a pure city or state authority does not have. **Relevance:** design-manual amendments do not go to the board; a standard that stays inside operator authority avoids the federal appointment vector entirely.

### 3. What is operator-fixed (MWAA's own instruments, adjustable on MWAA's timeline)

**MWAA Design Manual (August 2020, 7-volume).** "Mandatory guide … with the force of law on the Airport" [Source: https://www.mwaa.com/sites/mwaa.com/files/2021-09/01_DM_2020.pdf]. Current sections on acoustics, PA zoning, EVACS, FIDS, and tenant technology standards are a named gap that should be pulled from MWAA's engineering portal before drafting. This is the correct vehicle.

**2025 U&L (effective Jan 1, 2025; expires Dec 31, 2039).** Fifteen-year contract lock, with a $9.0B linked capital plan (context packet, §3). Specific MII thresholds are an unresolved gap.

**MWAA Design Review process** sits under the Chief Engineer / VP of Engineering. Amendments to the design manual are administrative; changes that materially affect rates or U&L obligations require board notice.

**Concourse E (IAD).** Design is largely frozen; construction underway; opens autumn 2026 (context packet, §4). **This building is not a real test bed for a new standard.** Post-opening measurement can, however, calibrate the standard: STIPA readings, ambient dBA, and reverberation time in a modern MWAA-built concourse would set an empirical baseline for the standard's thresholds.

### 4. The Air Carrier Access Act flank

14 CFR Part 382 (DOT ACAA) makes airlines — not just airports — responsible for effective communication at gates. This is worth flagging because the MWAA quiet-by-design standard cannot silence what airlines are separately required to communicate. Gate-agent boarding announcements, ADA-compliant boarding assistance calls, and irregular-operations messaging live inside airline obligations. **The standard can prescribe how loud, in which zone, and through which equipment — but cannot cut what airlines must communicate.**

