## Case Studies

**1. DCA slot expansion (2024): politics beat efficiency, again.** MWAA opposed beyond-perimeter expansion in writing. United Airlines, whose Washington hub is IAD, joined the "Coalition to Protect America's Regional Airports" against expansion. Delta and the "Capital Access Alliance" pushed for expansion. Congress delivered 10 slots — a compromise closer to the operator's position than the sponsor's ask [Source: https://www.everycrsreport.com/reports/IN12504.html]. **Read-across:** MWAA's institutional weight at Congress is real when it aligns with a plausible technical position (IAD has the capacity), and a well-constructed quiet-by-design standard that MWAA can point to as safety-and-accessibility-neutral will hold. A standard that reads as brand-work will not.

**2. London City "silent airport" policy: transferability is limited.** LCY publishes a policy that "announcements only [are] made in case of emergency" and directs passengers to screens [Source: https://www.londoncityairport.com/at-the-airport/need-to-know/airport-policies/silent-policy]. LCY is a UK regulator's airport, has no TSA presence, no CBP FIS, no NFPA 72 code adoption, no Part 139 emergency-plan overlay, no signatory-carrier lease structure, and a passenger profile heavily weighted to business travelers on European short-haul. **Read-across:** the LCY policy is a design intent, not a transferable operating model. The MWAA standard should cite it as a target for gate-level ambient conditions, not as an announcement-governance model.

**3. Saarinen main terminal historic preservation on the Dulles expansion.** The $20–22.5B program preserves Saarinen's main terminal explicitly; MWAA has expanded under the Virginia SHPO PMOA for decades [Source: https://parametric-architecture.com/trumps-22-5-billion-dulles-airport] [Source: https://www.achp.gov/sites/default/files/2021-03/2020_FAA_Section3_ProgressReport.pdf]. **Read-across:** acoustic interventions inside the main terminal envelope will be Section 106 / PMOA events. Assume 6–12 months of preservation consultation on any interior finish or ceiling change and plan schedule floats accordingly.

